   },
   "source": [
    "**Inline Question 1** \n",
    "\n",
    "Notice the structured patterns in the distance matrix, where some rows or columns are visibly brighter. (Note that with the default color scheme black indicates low distances while white indicates high distances.)\n",
    "\n",
    "- What in the data is the cause behind the distinctly bright rows?\n",
    "- What causes the columns?\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\color{blue}{\\textit Your Answer:}$ *fill this in.*\n",
    "\n",
    "- The bright rows represent the observations from classes that differ significantly from the vast majority of the training data, most likely due to its background color.\n",
    "- The bright columns represent the observations from classes that differ significantly from the vast majority of the test data, most likely due to its background color."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "id": "44c2cb31",
   "metadata": {
    "ExecuteTime": {
     "end_time": "2024-11-07T12:02:44.843343Z",
     "start_time": "2024-11-07T12:02:44.467315Z"
    }
   },
   "source": [
    "# Now implement the function predict_labels and run the code below:\n",
    "# We use k = 1 (which is Nearest Neighbor).\n",
    "y_test_pred = classifier.predict_labels(dists, k=1)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Compute and print the fraction of correctly predicted examples\n",
    "num_correct = np.sum(y_test_pred == y_test)\n",
    "accuracy = float(num_correct) / num_test\n",
    "print('Got %d / %d correct => accuracy: %f' % (num_correct, num_test, accuracy))"
   ],
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Got 137 / 500 correct => accuracy: 0.274000\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "execution_count": 29
  },
